<div><br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;That's what the first sentence in the above quote is abou=
t.<br>
</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>2. Signal whether a handshake message has been processed.<br>
</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;That's what the second sentence in the above quote is abo=
ut,<br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;which can also be rephased as follows:<br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;An ACK must only be sent for those records which consist =
of<br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;handshake messages each of which has been signalled as<br=
>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;processed by the handshake layer.<br>
</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;In particular, in situations where a single record contai=
ns<br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;multiple handshake messages/fragments, and the implementa=
tion<br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;processes only some of them, the record must not be ackno=
wledged.<br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;A real-world example for this would be an implementation =
which<br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;supports out-of-order buffering but only contiguous reass=
embly:<br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;If the initial fragment of a HS message gets sent in one =
record<br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;which is lost, and the final fragment together with a sub=
sequent<br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;handshake message is sent in another record, then this la=
tter<br>
</div>
<div>&nbsp; &nbsp;record must not be ACKed if the stack drops the fragment.=
<br>
</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>Unbuffered out-of-order handshake messages do trigger signal 1,<br>
</div>
<div>but they don't trigger signal 2.<br>
</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>At least, that's my understanding.<br>
</div>
<div><br>
</div>
<div>Best,<br>
</div>
<span>Hanno</span><br>
</div>
